# CICD-CTRL-3 — Dedicated Self-Hosted CI Runner

Heavy CI may execute on a dedicated, project-labelled self-hosted runner.
GitHub Actions stays the authoritative control plane.

Verify with `php artisan foundation:self-hosted-runner-check --strict`.

## Permanent rules

1. **The production VPS is never a general CI runner.** Deployment keeps its own
   boundary: `scripts/deploy-vps-runner.sh` runs ON the VPS and is never invoked
   from a CI job. `deploy-vps.sh` / `rollback-vps.sh` must not appear in the CI
   workflow.
2. **GitHub Actions remains authoritative.** A self-hosted runner adds execution
   capacity; it does not make CI independent of a GitHub Actions outage. Never
   claim otherwise.
3. **Heavy jobs target the full label set** `[self-hosted, linux, x64,
   daengtisia-ci]`. A bare `runs-on: self-hosted` is forbidden — any other runner
   on the account could satisfy it.
4. **The classifier stays GitHub-hosted.** A dead self-hosted runner must never
   stop the routing decision from being made.
5. **Exactly one critical-gate variant runs.** The GitHub-hosted and self-hosted
   variants share the check name `NSF-R011 Critical Test Gate` and have mutually
   exclusive conditions. Never let both run, and never let both skip.
6. **A runner outage queues the gate.** There is no automatic failover. A queued
   job is the correct, visible failure mode; a silently passing required check is
   not.
7. **Fallback is explicit and equivalent.** Operators flip the repository
   variable `CI_RUNNER_MODE` or pass the `runner_mode` dispatch input. The
   default is `github-hosted`. The fallback runs the same test filter and the
   same guards — never a weaker gate, never a renamed check.
8. **Fork pull requests never run on the dedicated runner.** They are routed to
   GitHub-hosted infrastructure — redirected, not skipped — so untrusted code
   never touches the runner or its secrets.
9. **CI uses a local, non-production database.** `php artisan
   ci:assert-non-production-database` runs before migrations in every DB-heavy
   job, on both runner types. It fails closed unless the database is local and
   carries a CI/test name.
10. **The runner runs CI as an unprivileged service user** (`github-runner`)
    under a managed systemd unit. Never root. Never left as an interactive
    `run.sh`. Never added to the `docker` group (root-equivalent) — the runner
    has no Docker, which is why the self-hosted variant uses local PostgreSQL
    instead of a service container.
11. **The runner holds no production material** — no production environment
    file, database credential, SSH private key, or application path. The health
    check reports PRESENT/ABSENT only and never prints a credential.
12. **The persistent workspace is cleaned** (`clean: true` checkout plus an
    explicit post-job cleanup scoped to `$GITHUB_WORKSPACE`). Package caches may
    persist; environment files, dumps, credentials, and clinical fixtures may
    not.
13. **Concurrency comes from a benchmark**, not from core count: one heavy job at
    a time, and a Pest worker count measured against swap, thermal, and database
    contention.
14. **Never weaken a gate to make CI faster.** Widening what the self-hosted
    runner may do requires extending `config/ci_runner.php`, the tests in
    `tests/Feature/Cicd/DedicatedSelfHostedRunnerTest.php`, and a passing
    `foundation:self-hosted-runner-check`.

## Files

- Contract: `config/ci_runner.php`
- Scanner: `app/Support/Cicd/SelfHostedRunnerScanner.php`
- Governance: `app/Services/Foundation/SelfHostedRunnerGovernanceService.php`
- Guard: `app/Console/Commands/CiAssertNonProductionDatabaseCommand.php`
- Health: `scripts/ci/self-hosted-runner-health.sh`
- Runbook: `docs/runbooks/self-hosted-runner.md`
